### Added

- `lighthouse serve` shows a Prioritering tab with a dependency graph, milestone progress and issues without a milestone.
- A workflow change now reaches origin without a run ever holding a token with `workflow` scope: a run whose branch touches `.github/workflows/` no longer pushes (`workflow-push-gate`) but hands off with `next: push-workflows`, and the tick starts `lighthouse push-workflows`. That process checks the worktree, roborev, a policy, `actionlint` and `zizmor`, has two or three reviewers in a `bwrap` sandbox judge a pre-merge workflow (in `shadow` mode by default: the answer `push` permits the commit), and then pushes with the workflow token from `[dispatch.workflow_push] token_file`. Without that table the route is off. The pusher also has a merge path, but the assumption that GitHub refuses such a merge with the run token is not verified. See `docs/tick.md` and `docs/install.md`.
- Scheduled job `map-refresh` (#121): a `[[schedule]]` block with `job = "map-refresh"` keeps the codebase map fresh. A Go gate measures the drift of `origin/<default branch>` since the last commit that touched `map_dir` (changed files under `drift_paths` plus a weight per new directory) and skips, runs the fast map or the full map; the result is checked for completeness (`documents`, `min_lines`), mirrored into `publish_dir` with an `index.md`, and lands through the auto-merged PR of the scheduled run. Invalid params are a config error logged at each tick. See `docs/tick.md`.
- The forge token can now be set per repo through `forge.token_env` instead of only `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN`, and triage, serve and ask open a forge per repo behind a forge interface.
- Lighthouse spreekt nu met GitLab: `[forge] kind = "gitlab"` (met `host` voor een zelf gehoste GitLab en `GITLAB_TOKEN`) opent een GitLab-client achter dezelfde forge-interface, `serve` toont de runs van zo'n repo en `run` accepteert subgroepen. De status van een issue komt uit de ingebouwde issuestatus van GitLab (*On hold* en *Controller* moeten op de bovenste groep van de lifecycle staan), en `[forge.board]` kent voor GitLab alleen `status_options` en `priority_options`. De Python-scripts van `pack/core` blijven voor GitLab ongeschikt tot een follow-up.
- `lighthouse forge` is het nieuwe oudercommando voor wat beide forges delen: issues, PR's en MR's, milestones, labels, blockers en `api` als uitwijkweg (in Evidence alleen GET), op GitHub en GitLab, zonder `merge`-commando en met de exitcodes 0, 1 en 2. De skill `lighthouse-forge` beschrijft per forge wat verschilt, en `lighthouse-start-issue`, `lighthouse-route-issue`, `lighthouse-write-issue` en `lighthouse-write-pr` gebruiken `lighthouse forge` in plaats van `gh`; `gh` blijft nodig voor `gh auth token` als terugval. De hooks (`pr-create-gate`) en `lighthouse evidence` werken op beide forges, en `board-start` draait zonder `gh`. De overige skills en scripts met `gh` volgen in vervolgissues.
- `lighthouse pins check` measures what lighthouse took over (skills, files, plugins and tools) against upstream and against what is installed, and `lighthouse init` reports local pin drift on stderr.
- `lighthouse pins lane` and the weekly `pins.yml` workflow turn each pin that is behind or cannot be measured into one draft PR (a clean merge of a skill or file, on `pins/<key>`) or one issue (a conflict, a plugin or tool behind its release, an error), without duplicates and without ever merging.
- `lighthouse report day|week|month` makes a report of merged PRs, closed issues, open PRs and new roadmap issues, enabled per repo with `[report] enabled = true`.
- `/lighthouse-upgrade` brings a repository to the version of the installed `lighthouse` binary: it offers `lighthouse self-update` when a newer release exists, shows the release notes in between, runs `lighthouse init --upgrade` in a worktree, checks the test gate, `check-instructions.sh` and `No changes.`, and commits only what the upgrade wrote. Push and PR follow only after your approval.
- `lighthouse agent-audit` lists the hooks, permission rules, skills, agents, rules and MCP servers of a repository, its user and its enabled plugins, each with its source, and reports conflicts between them and project hooks that overlap a lighthouse hook; it changes nothing. The first `lighthouse init`, and `lighthouse init --upgrade --audit`, end with a short summary, and the new skill `/lighthouse-audit-agent-config` judges each overlap candidate. See `docs/agent-audit.md`.

### Changed

- The shared instructions now say a branch that is already pushed is updated with `origin/<default branch>` by `git merge`, never by rebase plus force-push. `lighthouse-write-handoff` and `lighthouse-fix-pr` follow that rule.
- `pr-create-gate` runs in the `lighthouse` binary through `.claude/hooks/lighthouse/run.sh` and no longer needs `python3` or `jq`, and the skills `write-pr` and `close-issue` check and run Evidence rows with `lighthouse evidence check|run` and check a PR body outside Claude Code with `lighthouse pr-body-gate`, in place of `evidence.py` and `pr_body_gate.py`. Update the binary on your PATH before `lighthouse init --upgrade`: an older binary does not know these commands, and `pr-create-gate` then blocks every Bash call.
- `commit-test-gate` runs in the `lighthouse` binary through `.claude/hooks/lighthouse/run.sh`, and needs no `python3` or `jq` any more. Gate slots now also work on Windows. Update the binary on your PATH before `lighthouse init --upgrade`: an older binary does not know this hook.
- The skill scripts `board-start`, `post-artefact`, `unblock-dependents` and `worktree-audit` are now commands of the `lighthouse` binary (`lighthouse board-start`, and so on) and need no `python3` for them any more; the unblock workflow downloads the binary instead of installing `uv`. Update the binary on your PATH before `lighthouse init --upgrade`: an older binary does not know these commands.
  
  A `dev` manifest outside the lighthouse repository (for example a binary built by `go install @main`) now makes the unblock-dependents workflow fail, where the Python version worked. `lighthouse init --upgrade` removes `.lighthouse/lib/lighthouse_lib/{protocol,artefacts,pr_body_gate,git_invocations,repo_identity}.py`: that matters for any project script that imports one of them.
- `lighthouse check-instructions` replaces `.lighthouse/scripts/check-instructions.sh` and no longer needs `python3` or `sha256sum`. A repository that calls `.lighthouse/scripts/check-instructions.sh` from its own hooks or CI must switch to `lighthouse check-instructions`, because `init --upgrade` removes the script.
- De pakketten `go` en `node` leveren `gate_go.py` en `gate_node.py` niet meer mee: `[test_gate] command` roept `lighthouse gate go` en `lighthouse gate node` aan, met dezelfde uitvoer en exitcodes. `lighthouse init --upgrade` zet het oude commando om en meldt andere verwijzingen naar de scripts. De `lighthouse`-binary op het PATH moet deze release zijn, anders geeft de test-gate `unknown command "gate"`. `python3` staat niet meer in `requires.toml` van `go`, `node`, `shell` en `expo`.
- De Python-lib van `pack/core` (`.lighthouse/lib/`) en `create_labels.py` zijn weg: geen script van `pack/core` is nog Python. `lighthouse init --upgrade` ruimt ze op in een bestaande repo; een lokaal gewijzigd bestand onder `.lighthouse/lib/` blijft staan en wordt gemeld. Een host zonder `python3` werkt nu volledig, behalve voor het pakket `python` (`ty-gate`, `gate_python`, `python-standard`). De installatiegids vraagt Python niet meer als vereiste. Werk de binary op je PATH bij vóór `lighthouse init --upgrade`.
- `lighthouse serve` rebuilds the forge overview every 90 s with a 60 s time-out per build, and the page re-reads *Open questions* and *Returned* every 15 s, so a new question or return is on the page within the 3-minute tick interval.
- Every packaged skill now declares its origin (`metadata.origin`), so a pin check can tell the skills lighthouse wrote from the ones it took over.
- Breaking: a run on another kenn-forge node now keeps its content (carrier, transcript, markers, usage brake, stop and kill) on that node, reached through `lighthouse serve` with `[node] token_file`; every `[dispatch.nodes.<name>]` in `host.toml` now needs `serve_url` and `serve_token_file`, and the node's own `host.toml` needs `[dispatch.forge]` and `[dispatch.profiles]`. Without `[node]` the routes under `/api/node/v1/` stay 404, and `serve` does not start without a forge token. macOS example: `deploy/launchd/com.lighthouse.serve.plist`.
- A slot that a closing run frees in a tick now goes to the starts of that same tick, for `slots`, `max_software` and node slots; a parked run, a fix-pr handoff and a merge free nothing until the next tick.

### Fixed

- `lighthouse-close-issue` in controller mode asks one question when an acceptance criterion is still open after the merge (new work, split off or close), instead of ending silently and being handed back with *no handoff or question*.
- `lighthouse gate go` now selects the package of a deleted file or directory also when its path runs through a symlink, such as `/var` to `/private/var` on macOS.
- The transcript of a run in the devcontainer now lists its subagent transcripts by name, as on the host, also when the container's file system does not sort them, such as APFS on macOS.
- `go install` of a tag now serves the web UI instead of the placeholder.
- The controller no longer hands a run back when its workspace only runs ahead of `origin/<branch>`: the run starts on that HEAD, and a resume of the same session is never handed back for own work. `/lighthouse-write-handoff` now pushes the branch before the `🤖 Handoff` in controller mode and in the pause form, never the default branch.
